<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malawi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Malawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malawi is a landlocked country in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, lying along the western shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a>. Its name recalls the Maravi confederacy of the 16th and 17th centuries. It became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1891, renamed Nyasaland in 1907, was joined to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> in 1953, and became independent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Banda</a> in 1964.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malawi.jpg" alt="Malawi" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: Achim1999 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Malawi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Malawi is a landlocked country in Southeast Africa, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, Tanzania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">Great Rift Valley</a> runs through it from north to south, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a>, also called Lake Nyasa, makes up over three-quarters of its eastern boundary. The lake is about 587 kilometres long and 84 kilometres wide, and the Shire River flows from its southern end to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> in Mozambique. South of the lake lie the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shire-highlands">Shire Highlands</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zomba">Zomba</a> and Mulanje peaks rise. The altitude moderates what would otherwise be an equatorial climate, with rains between November and April.</p><p>The name Malawi means 'flames' in Chichewa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbuka-language">Chitumbuka</a> and was chosen by the first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Banda</a>, after independence in 1964. It comes from the Maravi, a Bantu ethnic group that emigrated from the southern Congo around 1400.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu peoples</a> from the north around the 10th century CE, the area had a small population of hunter-gatherers, known in oral tradition as Akafula or BaTwa, who were gradually overtaken by agrarian Bantu peoples. By the 16th century the Bantu groups had established kingdoms such as the Maravi Empire, which reached from north of Nkhotakota to the Zambezi River, and the Nkhamanga. In the 17th century, with the area mostly united under one ruler, people began trading and making alliances with Portuguese traders and soldiers. By the 18th century the empire had broken up.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> slave trade reached its height in the mid-19th century, when about 20,000 people a year are believed to have been enslaved and taken from Nkhotakota to Kilwa. David Livingstone reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a> in 1859 and identified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shire-highlands">Shire Highlands</a> as suitable for European settlement.</p><p>Anglican and Presbyterian missions followed Livingstone's visit in the 1860s and 1870s, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-lakes-company">African Lakes Company</a> was established in 1878, and a British consul took up residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blantyre">Blantyre</a> in 1883. To prevent Portuguese occupation, the British government sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Harry Johnston</a> as consul to make treaties with local rulers. In 1891 the area became the British Central African Protectorate, renamed Nyasaland in 1907. Nyasaland became independent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Banda</a> in 1964.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Malawi is the Republic of Malawi, with its capital and largest city at Lilongwe and its commercial centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blantyre">Blantyre</a>. English is the official language, and Chichewa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbuka-language">Chitumbuka</a> and Chiyao are major regional languages. Lake Malawi National Park, listed in 1984, and the Chongoni Rock Art Area, listed in 2006, are its two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malawi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The caravan routes it financed carried Islam, the Swahili language, firearms, cloth and new crops deep into the interior, linking the peoples of present-day Tanzania, Kenya, Uganda, <b>Malawi</b> and the eastern Congo to the Indian Ocean economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It spread as a church hymn and as an anthem of political meetings, and independence movements across southern Africa sang it, including in Chichewa in <b>Malawi</b> and Zambia.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a></li><li><blockquote>Its borders became those of modern <b>Malawi</b>, and its planter economy and migrant labour persisted into the colonial period.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-central-africa-protectorate">British Central Africa Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1850s the Ngoni under the successors of Zwangendaba invaded northern <b>Malawi</b> and destroyed the kingdom, incorporating many Tumbuka.</blockquote><a href="/state/chikulamayembe-state">Chikulamayembe State</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Malawi</b> and Zambia became independent in 1964, while Southern Rhodesia moved towards unilateral independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a></li><li><blockquote>The name Maravi survives in modern <b>Malawi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-maravi">Kingdom of Maravi</a></li></ul></section>
